========================================================================
PART D -- an actual search for Jacobian pairs over F_p
========================================================================

D0.  PROVED lemma (characteristic p).  For every P in F_p[x,y] and every
     R in F_p[x^p,y^p]:  R_x = R_y = 0, hence Jac(P,R) = 0.  The set of
     Jacobian mates of P is therefore a coset closed under adding F_p[x^p,y^p]:
     mate degrees are UNBOUNDED, and (n,m) 'admits a Jacobian pair' over F_p
     for infinitely many m as soon as it does for one.
  [OK ] D0 Frobenius kernel lemma:  Jac(P, F_p[x^p,y^p]) = 0  (160 random instances) 
     CONSEQUENCE: the honest characteristic-p invariants are
       d_min(P) = MINIMAL degree of a Jacobian mate  (the 'primitive' partner degree)
       d_max(P) = MAXIMAL degree of a Jacobian mate  (what (n,m) is 'realised')
     A char-p pair at (n,m) with d_min < m is a Frobenius/C[P] inflation of the
     primitive pair (n,d_min) and is NOT evidence about JC(2) in characteristic 0.
     Explicit witness: P = x^2+y over F_5 has the mate Q = x^5-x of degree 5,
     Jac(P,Q) = 1, and (P,Q) is NOT an automorphism (Artin-Schreier), while
     d_min(P) = 1 with the automorphism mate Q = -x.
  [OK ] D0b witness (x^2+y, x^5-x) over F_5: Jac = 1 
  [OK ] D0c witness is NOT an automorphism, while its primitive reduction (2,1) is 

